Bounded failure diagnosis
Unchanged frozen14 WebSocketLifecycleTests.swift:225 still configures LifecyclePeer accepted sockets with timeoutSeconds:1. JPIPTCPListener passes this argument to the accepted connection's receive/send timeout; it is not listener polling. A fixture intended to hold until explicit release/close can therefore expire an idle connection before a delayed request reaches it. This is the same source-level lifetime mismatch previously corrected in the separate HTTP close peer, and is actionable in this fixture too.
The native failure records do not identify which of the first-request, parked-cleanup, replacement-handshake, cleanup-acknowledgement, reader-close or peer-finish predicates timed out. They also do not record the peer's recv errno. Consequently, the mismatch is confirmed but attribution of these exact three failures remains unproved. Source cleanup closes listener and captured connections, releases the handshake gate, and observes worker completion; the catch path's try? eventually is a bounded observation, not an unconditional join claim. A narrowly authorised fixture correction can remove only this incidental socket expiry and label failing phases while preserving all two-second/5ms barrier settings, real request deadlines, adversarial branches and generation assertions. It is not a production deadline change or an automatic waiver; a successor must execute the required gates.

